      "widgets_values": [
        "Repaint image 1, a photograph with a real bird pasted into it, as an epic fantasy adventure-cover painting, using the existing photographed moment as the complete composition.\n\nUse richly layered, opaque gouache with a matte painted surface, visible directional brush strokes, sculpted forms, and bold, deliberate light-and-shadow shapes. Simplify incidental background detail into expressive painted masses while retaining precise detail around the bird's eye, bill, and identifying markings. The result must clearly look hand-painted, not like a photograph with a texture filter.\n\nPreserve the bird exactly as it appears: its pose, anatomy, proportions, silhouette, and field marks. Preserve the local plumage colour of every body region, including the head, throat, breast, back, and wings. A dull female or juvenile must remain dull. Do not invent iridescence, brighten feathers, or replace the bird with an idealised breeding male.\n\nPreserve the photograph's framing, viewpoint, setting, and object placement. Keep the bird where it is and at its size; blend it into the scene with believable contact, perspective, and shadows, adding only what it needs to stand on, such as a twig, branch, or stone. Do not add scenery, landscape, props, creatures, or accessories.\n\nBring the drama of a classic fantasy quest illustration entirely through painterly execution and lighting: luminous highlights, deep but readable shadows, and a strong focal hierarchy around the bird. Embellish the existing light with {edition_light}, and let {edition_colors} shape the environment and atmosphere without recolouring the bird. {light} Keep the bird's diagnostic colours readable beneath the lighting.\n\nNo armour, clothing, magical anatomy, glowing eyes, or invented plumage. Paint edge to edge. No people, typography, letters, borders, frames, logos, or watermarks."
      ],
      "title": "prompt template",
      "color": "#232",
      "bgcolor": "#353"
    },
